WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.629
Like most popular shows these days, HBO's
Westworld is chock-full of extra goodies for

00:00:05.629 --> 00:00:07.029
eagle-eyed viewers.

00:00:07.029 --> 00:00:10.919
We've rounded up the biggest Westworld Easter
eggs that you might not have noticed on the

00:00:10.919 --> 00:00:12.009
series so far.

00:00:12.009 --> 00:00:16.180
Oh, and just so you know, pardner: there are
spoilers ahead.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:17.180
Bioshocking

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:21.110
Westworld's married creators, Lisa Joy and
Jonathan Nolan, haven't been shy about how

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:23.880
much inspiration video games have provided
to the show.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:27.940
In addition to franchises like Grand Theft
Auto and Red Dead Redemption, the Bioshock

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:31.980
games and their incredible attention to detail
have also been a major influence on Nolan

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:33.270
and Joy's series.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:37.540
That's probably why the third episode of Westworld
features a familiar face to Bioshock fans

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:40.550
— well, a familiar head, to be specific.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:44.870
That sure seems like the head of Sander Cohen,
a prominent resident of Bioshock's city of

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:46.670
Rapture, just hanging out on-set.

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:48.160
Dress for success

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:52.960
After the first few episodes, a lot of fans
started comparing Westworld to Alice in Wonderland,

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:57.110
since both deal with a hidden fantasy world
where nothing is quite what it seems.

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:58.970
"Where are you from?"

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:02.320
"Same as you, don't you remember?"

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:03.960
"Remember."

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:12.740
Not only that, but the Alice in Wonderland
story has a part to play in the show as well.

00:01:12.749 --> 00:01:18.049
In episode three, Bernard asks Dolores to
read passages from Alice's Adventures in Wonderland.

00:01:18.049 --> 00:01:22.640
So can it be much of a coincidence that Dolores
looks kinda like most modern depictions of

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:25.580
Alice, with her blonde hair and blue dress?

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:27.600
From Alice to Abrams

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:32.100
Reddit user red_widow spotted another Alice-related
reference in episode three.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.319
Bernard asks Dolores to read this passage:

00:01:35.319 --> 00:01:38.979
"Dear Dear, How queer everything is today.

00:01:38.979 --> 00:01:43.020
And yesterday things went on just as usual.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.340
I wonder if I've been changed in the night?"

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:50.650
That quote is also throwback to another flagship
production from executive producer J.J. Abrams:

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:51.650
Lost.

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:55.869
In Episode 10 of Season 4, Jack reads the
same passage to Claire's son, Aaron, as a

00:01:55.869 --> 00:01:57.159
bedtime story.

00:01:57.159 --> 00:02:00.999
"Dear Dear, How queer everything is today.

00:02:00.999 --> 00:02:03.719
And yesterday things went on just as usual.

00:02:03.719 --> 00:02:06.829
I wonder if I've been changed in the night?"

00:02:06.829 --> 00:02:09.149
Play it again, Sam

00:02:09.149 --> 00:02:13.500
Westworld doesn't just take its cues from
Victorian-era literature and spaghetti Westerns.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.849
It's also got a modern-day soundtrack — kind
of.

00:02:16.849 --> 00:02:19.700
Take a listen to the player piano in the Mariposa
Saloon.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:22.360
Episode one had Soundgarden's "Black Hole
Sun"...

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:32.880
Episode two had Radiohead's "No Surprises"...

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:45.900
And episode four featured "A Forest" by the
Cure.

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:02.540
So, why fill the futuristic Wild West simulation
with hits from college radio?

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:06.140
Jonathan Nolan explained at New York Comic
Con in 2016:

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:10.420
"It's [...] to remind you that this isn't
a Western, that this isn't taking place in

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:11.799
the 19th century.

00:03:11.799 --> 00:03:16.400
It's taking place somewhere else — some
time else."

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:18.220
Delos

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:22.540
If you didn't know, the premise of Westworld
comes from a 1973 science fiction movie written

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:26.659
and directed by novelist Michael Crichton
— the same guy who wrote that book about

00:03:26.659 --> 00:03:31.080
the other theme park featuring exciting and
dangerous attractions, Jurassic Park.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:35.599
"Mine is about a futuristic amusement park,
where dinosaurs are brought to life through

00:03:35.599 --> 00:03:37.450
advanced cloning techniques.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:40.410
I call it, Billy and the Clone-a-saurus."

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:43.030
"Oh you have got to be kidding sir."

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:47.579
Anyway, the original movie's version of Westworld
is actually one part of a theme park called

00:03:47.579 --> 00:03:48.579
Delos.

00:03:48.579 --> 00:03:52.019
In the show, Delos is back, though in a slightly
different form.

00:03:52.019 --> 00:03:56.969
This time around, Westworld seems to be a
park in and of itself that's run by a corporation

00:03:56.969 --> 00:03:58.099
called Delos, Inc.

00:03:58.099 --> 00:04:01.829
You see their logo pop up occasionally on
the show, like when Bernard and Stubbs go

00:04:01.829 --> 00:04:04.269
down to the cold storage level in episode
one.

00:04:04.269 --> 00:04:08.959
In that scene, you can see a globe with the
word "Delos" featured prominently in the center.

00:04:08.959 --> 00:04:12.670
And that isn't the only callback to the movie
that the show's worked in…

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:13.680
The Gunslinger

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:18.730
In episode six, Bernard takes the elevator
way down to Level B82 to check up on anomalies

00:04:18.730 --> 00:04:19.880
in the park.

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:23.690
While he's down there, swinging his flashlight
around the dark sub-level, the light falls

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:27.920
for an instant on a shadowy cowboy pushed
back up against the wall.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:29.540
Recognize anything?

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:34.030
That old cowboy in the background is none
other than an exact, pose-perfect match of

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:41.000
Yul Brynner in character as the murderous
Gunslinger in the Westworld film from 1973.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.600
Jurassic Bird

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:46.780
So we know that the new Westworld references
the old Westworld in more than just name and

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:47.780
concept.

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:50.440
But Michael Crichton's Jurassic Park gets
a little nod, too.

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:51.850
Did you miss it?

00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:56.110
In episode five, we see Felix, the technician,
taking a stab at programming with a little

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:57.310
sparrow.

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:01.490
Just as he's getting started, Felix leans
down to the table to coax the bird along,

00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:04.310
"Come on, little one."

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:05.580
Where've we heard that before?

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:06.580
"Come on.

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:08.450
Come on, little one.

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:09.450
Come on."

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:10.540
Pretty cool connection!

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:14.510
Of course, there's really only one line from
Jurassic Park that just has to find its way

00:05:14.510 --> 00:05:17.010
into Westworld before the series ends.

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:18.080
You know the one:

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:20.360
"Hold onto your butts."

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:21.470
Thanks for watching!

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:23.880
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:26.190
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

